<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · region</p><h1 class="enc-title">Izu Province</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Izu Province was an old province of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/japan">Japan</a> covering the Izu Peninsula and the Izu Islands, now part of Shizuoka Prefecture and Tokyo. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/minamoto-no-yoritomo">Minamoto no Yoritomo</a> lived there in exile before raising his revolt in 1180. In the 1490s <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hojo-soun">Ise Moritoki</a>, later called Hojo Soun, seized Izu, the first base of the Later <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hojo-clan">Hojo clan</a>, and the peninsula is known for its hot springs.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/izu-province.jpg" alt="Izu Province" width="320" height="335" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Ash_Crow · CC BY 2.5 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Provinces_of_Japan-Izu.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Izu Province, known in its abbreviated form as Zushu, bordered Sagami and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/suruga-province">Suruga Provinces</a>. Its mainland part, the Izu Peninsula, is today the eastern portion of Shizuoka Prefecture, while the Izu Islands belong to Tokyo. The province was created in 680 CE, when two districts of Suruga Province, Tagata and Kamo, were separated to form it. Naka District was added at some point between 701 and 710. The capital was at Mishima, which also held the province's Kokubun-ji and its Ichinomiya, the Mishima Taisha. Under the Engishiki classification Izu ranked as a lesser country.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>Under the ritsuryo legal system Izu was one of the preferred places of exile for those convicted of political crimes by the Heian period court. In the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kamakura">Kamakura</a> period it was ruled by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hojo-clan">Hojo clan</a>. In the Muromachi period the Uesugi clan held it nominally through their post as Kanto Kanrei, but in practice it came under whoever ruled Sagami and Musashi, which by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sengoku-period">Sengoku period</a> meant the Later Hojo clan based at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/odawara">Odawara</a>. After the Battle of Odawara, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/toyotomi-hideyoshi">Toyotomi Hideyoshi</a> moved <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tokugawa-ieyasu">Tokugawa Ieyasu</a> from the Tokai region to the Kanto, and Izu came under Tokugawa rule.</p><p>Under the Tokugawa shogunate Izu stayed a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tenryo">tenryo</a> territory administered directly by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shogun">shogun</a>, much of it by a daikan at Nirayama, with portions given to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hatamoto">hatamoto</a> and to Odawara Domain. Kimisawa District was added to the three ancient districts. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tokaido">Tokaido road</a> between Edo and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kyoto">Kyoto</a> crossed northern Izu, with a post station at Mishima-shuku, and the port of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shimoda">Shimoda</a> in the south was a required port of call for vessels approaching Edo from the east.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shimoda">Shimoda</a> was chosen by the Tokugawa government as a port to be opened to American trade under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/convention-of-kanagawa">Convention of Kanagawa</a>, signed by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/matthew-c-perry">Commodore Matthew Perry</a> on 31 March 1854. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yoshida-shoin">Yoshida Shoin</a> made an unsuccessful attempt there in 1854 to board Perry's Black Ships. The first American consulate in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/japan">Japan</a> opened at the temple of Gyokusen-ji in Shimoda under Townsend Harris, who negotiated the Treaty of Amity and Commerce, signed at Ryosen-ji in 1858. The Treaty of Shimoda with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a> was signed at Choraku-ji in 1855.</p><p>After the start of the Meiji period Izu was merged into the short-lived Ashigaru Prefecture in 1871. That prefecture was divided between Shizuoka Prefecture and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kanagawa-prefecture">Kanagawa Prefecture</a> on 18 April 1876, and the Izu Islands passed from Shizuoka to Tokyo in 1878.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Izu Province” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Ise Moritoki, later called Hojo Soun, a minor official with ties to the Imagawa, seized <b>Izu</b> in the 1490s and took Odawara castle, traditionally in 1495.</blockquote><a href="/state/later-hojo-clan-domain">Later Hojo clan domain</a></li></ul></section>
